WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.574
One more picture from the shop.

2
00:00:04.574 --> 00:00:06.509
By mid morning it's at its best.

3
00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:12.192
The shelves are full, the coffee machine is hot, and the shopkeeper knows where everything is.

4
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:18.181
Now imagine taking a photo of that moment, and the next day, stepping straight into it.

5
00:00:18.181 --> 00:00:20.307
Shop and all, exactly as it was.

6
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:24.306
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:24.306 --> 00:00:26.578
My JVM gets fast once it warms up.

8
00:00:26.578 --> 00:00:30.853
So why can't I just save it warm, and start from there tomorrow?

9
00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:32.733
As it turns out, you can.

10
00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:35.133
But the photo has one awkward limit.

11
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.984
First, what warm means.

12
00:00:37.984 --> 00:00:47.325
The lesson's WarmupDemo runs the same work ten times in a fresh JVM: 20,000 check digit tests on tracking numbers.

13
00:00:47.325 --> 00:00:52.568
Round 1 took 125 milliseconds, and every round after it about 5.

14
00:00:52.568 --> 00:00:54.862
Nothing in the code changed.

15
00:00:54.862 --> 00:00:55.845
The JVM did.

16
00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:59.063
A fresh JVM interprets and counts.

17
00:00:59.063 --> 00:01:02.711
Then C1, its quick compiler, compiles the hot methods.

18
00:01:02.711 --> 00:01:07.169
Then C2, the thorough one, compiles them again, using the profile.

19
00:01:07.169 --> 00:01:11.019
That climb is warm-up, and every new JVM climbs it again.

20
00:01:11.019 --> 00:01:16.896
In the shop, it's learning the popular items by heart, and forgetting them every night.

21
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.431
So let's take the photo.

22
00:01:19.431 --> 00:01:24.121
CRaC, Coordinated Restore at Checkpoint, does that for a running JVM.

23
00:01:24.121 --> 00:01:27.112
A checkpoint writes the running JVM to disk.

24
00:01:27.112 --> 00:01:30.307
A restore starts a new process from that image.

25
00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:31.475
Let's map it.

26
00:01:31.475 --> 00:01:35.820
The busy mid morning shop is a warm JVM, after real traffic.

27
00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.848
The photo is the checkpoint.

28
00:01:37.848 --> 00:01:40.672
Stepping into the photo is the restore.

29
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.892
Under the hood, a Linux tool called CRIU freezes the process.

30
00:01:45.892 --> 00:01:58.275
It writes the memory pages, each thread's CPU registers, the processor's own small storage slots, and the process's state in the operating system, into a folder of image files.

31
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:06.424
A restore builds a new process from those files, and it carries on at the very instruction where the old one stopped.

32
00:02:06.424 --> 00:02:11.672
The heap, the loaded classes, and the JIT's compiled code all come back as they were.

33
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.713
Now a prediction.

34
00:02:13.713 --> 00:02:18.822
At the moment of the photo, the service has a connection open to its database.

35
00:02:18.822 --> 00:02:22.032
After the restore, is that connection still good?

36
00:02:22.032 --> 00:02:23.080
Pause and guess.

37
00:02:25.867 --> 00:02:26.063
No.

38
00:02:26.063 --> 00:02:29.736
A network connection lives partly on the other computer.

39
00:02:29.736 --> 00:02:33.343
The database dropped its side long before your restore.

40
00:02:33.343 --> 00:02:38.459
The photo shows you holding the phone, but the other person hung up yesterday.

41
00:02:39.367 --> 00:02:43.070
Files are risky too: a file may have changed, or moved.

42
00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:47.648
So CRaC refuses to checkpoint while files or sockets are still open.

43
00:02:47.648 --> 00:02:52.293
A socket is one end of a network connection, as your program sees it.

44
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.089
That's the coordinated part of the name.

45
00:02:55.089 --> 00:03:03.116
The JVM tells your code just before the checkpoint, so it can let go, and just after the restore, so it can reach out again.

46
00:03:04.033 --> 00:03:06.199
The whole flow is three commands.

47
00:03:06.199 --> 00:03:09.809
Start the service, allowing a checkpoint into a folder.

48
00:03:09.809 --> 00:03:15.387
After some warm-up traffic, ask jcmd, the JDK's command tool, to take the checkpoint.

49
00:03:15.387 --> 00:03:18.210
That writes the image and ends the process.

50
00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:21.688
Later, restore from the folder, as often as you like.

51
00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:26.168
There's one trap in those lines, and jcmd is the cheerful culprit.

52
00:03:26.168 --> 00:03:30.048
It prints, command executed successfully, whatever happened.

53
00:03:30.048 --> 00:03:34.187
It's the friend who says the exam went great, every single time.

54
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:39.009
So the point is: the truth is in the application's own console.

55
00:03:39.009 --> 00:03:40.472
Read that, not jcmd.

56
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.543
How fast is a restore?

57
00:03:43.543 --> 00:03:54.647
The CRaC project published these times to first operation in 2020, from an early CRaC build of JDK 14 on a laptop.

58
00:03:54.647 --> 00:03:59.907
Spring Boot: 3,898 milliseconds normally, 38 restored.

59
00:03:59.907 --> 00:04:02.342
Micronaut: 1,001, and 46.

60
00:04:02.342 --> 00:04:04.388
Quarkus: 980, and 33.

61
00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:08.583
Here the photo picture breaks, and the break matters.

62
00:04:08.583 --> 00:04:11.123
You can't step into the same photo twice.

63
00:04:11.123 --> 00:04:17.565
But you can restore one CRaC image into ten processes, and all ten wake up with exactly the same memory.

64
00:04:17.767 --> 00:04:20.783
Ten shopkeepers, each sure she's the only one.

65
00:04:20.783 --> 00:04:26.029
Anything that should differ in each copy, like a random id, comes back the same.

66
00:04:26.029 --> 00:04:29.243
So each copy makes a fresh one after the restore.

67
00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:31.525
Now the guardrails.

68
00:04:31.525 --> 00:04:40.531
One: CRaC isn't part of mainline OpenJDK, so you need a vendor's CRaC build, such as Azul Zulu or BellSoft Liberica, on Linux.

69
00:04:40.531 --> 00:04:44.462
Two: CRIU needs extra rights from the operating system.

70
00:04:44.462 --> 00:04:51.324
Three: a restore on a different CPU can fail, unless you pin the CPU features at the checkpoint.

71
00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:57.400
Four: the image holds the whole heap, secrets included, so store it like a heap dump.

72
00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:04.456
And the rule to remember: a checkpoint saves everything inside the process, and nothing outside it.

73
00:05:04.456 --> 00:05:10.239
Your code lets go and reaches out again through two hooks, beforeCheckpoint and afterRestore.

74
00:05:11.167 --> 00:05:14.457
So, can you save a warm JVM and start from there?

75
00:05:14.457 --> 00:05:14.725
Yes.

76
00:05:14.725 --> 00:05:21.708
Warm it up, take the photo, and step into it as often as you like, at full speed from the first request.

77
00:05:21.708 --> 00:05:25.401
Just hang up the phone before you smile for the camera.

78
00:05:25.633 --> 00:05:26.484
Quick check.

79
00:05:26.484 --> 00:05:30.951
Your service keeps a log file open, and nobody wrote any hooks.

80
00:05:30.951 --> 00:05:33.079
You ask jcmd for a checkpoint.

81
00:05:33.079 --> 00:05:36.199
What does jcmd say, and what really happens?

82
00:05:38.967 --> 00:05:41.611
jcmd says command executed successfully.

83
00:05:41.611 --> 00:05:45.378
But the checkpoint fails, because the file is still open.

84
00:05:45.378 --> 00:05:49.675
The application's console says so, and the process keeps running.
